# Hostile audit — inactive-container augmentation obstruction

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** audited-working proof-only negative theorem; theorem state unchanged  
**Audited source:** [inactive-container augmentation obstruction](target_c_inactive_container_augmentation_obstruction.md)

## Verdict

The construction is a valid negative model theorem. It proves that the four coarse
container statistics used in the Type-I branch—quadratic area, quadratic isolated
population, linear perimeter, and linear Oler slack—do not force even one additional
unit-separated point. The examples are not claimed to be global minimizers and do not
remove the source-specific coupling required by the actual C-II branch.

## Checks

1. For a compact convex body, the maximum unit-separated packing number exists by
   compactness. Oler supplies the upper bound, while translation averaging of one unit
   triangular lattice supplies the lower bound N(C)>=area(C)/a_0.

2. If N(lambda C)>=N(C)+1 for a sequence lambda downarrow1, rescaling the extra
   packings back into C and taking a compact subsequential limit would produce
   N(C)+1 unit-separated points in C. Hence the first augmentation threshold
   lambda_* is strictly above one for each fixed body.

3. For a disk C_R, choose epsilon_R below half that threshold gap and below R^{-2}.
   The scaled maximum packing remains of cardinality N_R, lies strictly inside the
   enlarged disk, and all pair distances are at least 1+epsilon_R/2. Thus every
   point is contact-isolated while no packing of one larger cardinality exists in the
   enlarged container.

4. The enlarged disk has area Theta(R^2) and perimeter O(R). Lattice averaging gives
   N_R=Omega(R^2). Since epsilon_R<=R^{-2}, the area increase is O(1), and the
   enlarged-disk Oler slack is bounded by its perimeter term plus a constant, hence
   O(R).

5. The conclusion is deliberately only a counterexample to statistics-only
   augmentation. It does not exclude a theorem using the actual core boundary,
   contact-wall guards, a protected collar, or the diameter graph.

**Claim classification:** audited-working proof-only negative model theorem; theorem state
unchanged; C-II remains open.

